An object **encapsulates state and behaviour**. The state is held in the **data members** (member variables, instance variables, attributes); the behaviour is given by the **member methods**. Each object has its own copy of the data members.

```java
class SmartCard {
    int cardNo;
    double balance;
    int trips;

    void setCard(int no) {
        cardNo = no;
    }

    void recharge(double amount) {
        double bonus = 0;
        if (amount >= 500)
            bonus = 25;
        balance = balance + amount + bonus;
    }

    boolean travel(double fare) {
        if (balance >= fare) {
            balance = balance - fare;
            trips++;
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Card " + cardNo + ": balance " + balance + ", trips " + trips);
    }
}
```

SmartCard has 3 data members (`cardNo`, `balance`, `trips`) and 4 member methods (`setCard`, `recharge`, `travel`, `show`).

Running `c.setCard(4501); c.recharge(500); c.travel(35.5); c.show();` on a new card prints `Card 4501: balance 489.5, trips 1`.

- A **data member** is declared in the class; it lives as long as its object and gets a default value.
- A **parameter** is declared in the method heading and takes the value of the argument of the call.
- A **local variable** is declared in the body of a method; it is created at every call, lost when the call ends, and has **no default value** (javac: "variable total might not have been initialized").

In `recharge` of SmartCard, `bonus` is local and `amount` is a parameter. In Turnstile, the local variable `today` starts again at every call while the data member `entries` keeps counting: three calls print `1 1 / 1 2 / 1 3`.

```java
class Turnstile {
    int entries;

    void enter() {
        int today = 0;
        today++;
        entries++;
        System.out.println(today + " " + entries);
    }
}
```

A **class** is a **user-defined type** and a **blueprint** for a set of objects. It is an **abstraction**: only the attributes and actions that the program needs are modelled (a fare program keeps `cardNo`, `balance` and `trips` of a metro card, not its colour).

A class is also an **object factory**: every use of `new` makes one more **object**, an **instance** of the class, with its own copy of the data members. Two seed packets from one class show `Tomato: 50 seeds, Rs 25.0 / Marigold: 120 seeds, Rs 30.0`.

```java
class SeedPacket {
    String variety;
    int seeds;
    double price;

    void set(String v, int s, double p) {
        variety = v;
        seeds = s;
        price = p;
    }

    double costPerSeed() {
        return price / seeds;
    }

    boolean cheaperThan(SeedPacket other) {
        return costPerSeed() < other.costPerSeed();
    }

    void show() {
        System.out.println(variety + ": " + seeds + " seeds, Rs " + price);
    }
}
```

```java
SeedPacket p = new SeedPacket();
p.set("Tomato", 50, 25.0);
p.show();
```

The pattern is `ClassName variable = new ClassName();`. The declaration alone makes only a **reference variable**; using it gives "variable p might not have been initialized". `new` creates the object, sets default values and returns its **reference**, which is stored in the variable. Then `object.method()` sends a message to the object.

To count the objects of a program, count the executions of `new`. Values are given to an object with an `accept` or `set` method; constructors are studied in a later chapter.

A hostel room of 4 beds, after `admit(3)` and `admit(3)`, prints `3 1 0` (admitted, admitted, vacancy).

**Primitive** types (`int`, `double`, `char`, `boolean` and others) are built into Java; a variable holds the **value**, and the size is fixed (int 4 bytes, double 8 bytes). **Composite** (class, reference) types are defined by a class; a variable holds the **reference** of an object, and the size of the object depends on its data members. `String` is a built-in composite type; a class such as `Plot` is a user-defined one.

Default values of data members: 0, 0.0, false for primitives and `null` for class types. For a new Plot the five members print `0 / 0.0 / false / null / null`. A local variable has no default.

